1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,360
In the last video, we found we could

2
00:00:01,960 --> 00:00:06,240
predict how fast a chip turns out tokens

3
00:00:04,360 --> 00:00:08,760
from just two numbers.

4
00:00:06,240 --> 00:00:10,880
How fast it does arithmetic in

5
00:00:08,760 --> 00:00:13,320
operations per second,

6
00:00:10,880 --> 00:00:16,080
and how fast it moves bytes between

7
00:00:13,320 --> 00:00:17,760
memory and the compute units in bytes

8
00:00:16,080 --> 00:00:20,080
per second.

9
00:00:17,760 --> 00:00:23,000
From those two alone, we landed on the

10
00:00:20,080 --> 00:00:25,200
central puzzle of LLM inference.

11
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:27,840
Generating a single token barely uses

12
00:00:25,200 --> 00:00:31,160
the arithmetic at all. Less than 1% of

13
00:00:27,840 --> 00:00:33,240
the time goes to math. The other 99% is

14
00:00:31,160 --> 00:00:36,200
the chip sitting idle waiting for the

15
00:00:33,240 --> 00:00:39,280
model's weights to arrive from memory.

16
00:00:36,200 --> 00:00:43,800
Decode wasn't compute bound. It was

17
00:00:39,280 --> 00:00:45,200
memory bound by a factor of almost 300.

18
00:00:43,800 --> 00:00:47,280
Last video was about what that

19
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:49,520
bottleneck is. This one is about the

20
00:00:47,280 --> 00:00:52,080
machine that creates it.

21
00:00:49,520 --> 00:00:53,840
We're going to open the GPU up and ask

22
00:00:52,080 --> 00:00:56,160
three questions.

23
00:00:53,840 --> 00:00:58,480
Why is it built so differently from the

24
00:00:56,160 --> 00:01:00,960
processor in your laptop?

25
00:00:58,480 --> 00:01:03,840
Why does moving a byte across it cost so

26
00:01:00,960 --> 00:01:06,400
much more than doing arithmetic on one?

27
00:01:03,840 --> 00:01:10,080
And once we understand that, what can

28
00:01:06,400 --> 00:01:12,160
the hardware actually do to fight back?

29
00:01:10,080 --> 00:01:14,320
It starts with a single bet about the

30
00:01:12,160 --> 00:01:16,240
kind of work this chip would spend its

31
00:01:14,320 --> 00:01:18,000
life doing. [music]

32
00:01:16,240 --> 00:01:20,640
The bet is this.

33
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:23,200
The work this chip will do all day is

34
00:01:20,640 --> 00:01:25,400
the same few arithmetic operations

35
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:26,880
repeated across billions of numbers at

36
00:01:25,400 --> 00:01:29,600
once.

37
00:01:26,880 --> 00:01:32,120
That is the shape of a matrix multiply,

38
00:01:29,600 --> 00:01:34,680
and a transformer is mostly matrix

39
00:01:32,120 --> 00:01:36,880
multiplies. Design a chip around that

40
00:01:34,680 --> 00:01:38,400
one assumption, and it ends up looking

41
00:01:36,880 --> 00:01:39,960
nothing like the processor in your

42
00:01:38,400 --> 00:01:42,840
laptop.

43
00:01:39,960 --> 00:01:45,400
A CPU is built to rush a single thread

44
00:01:42,840 --> 00:01:47,520
through as fast as it can, even when

45
00:01:45,400 --> 00:01:48,880
that thread keeps making unpredictable

46
00:01:47,520 --> 00:01:50,960
turns.

47
00:01:48,880 --> 00:01:53,800
So, most of its silicon goes not into

48
00:01:50,960 --> 00:01:55,720
doing arithmetic, but into anticipation,

49
00:01:53,800 --> 00:01:57,560
guessing what the thread will do next,

50
00:01:55,720 --> 00:01:59,880
and lining up the data before the thread

51
00:01:57,560 --> 00:02:01,360
asks for it, so it almost never has to

52
00:01:59,880 --> 00:02:03,760
stop and wait.

53
00:02:01,360 --> 00:02:07,040
A chip built that way runs maybe a few

54
00:02:03,760 --> 00:02:10,200
tens of those fast threads at once.

55
00:02:07,040 --> 00:02:12,760
A GPU makes the opposite trade.

56
00:02:10,200 --> 00:02:14,520
Nvidia's H100, the data center chip

57
00:02:12,760 --> 00:02:17,600
we'll use throughout, is built on

58
00:02:14,520 --> 00:02:20,880
Hopper, its 2022 architecture.

59
00:02:17,600 --> 00:02:24,600
It spends its silicon on 132 identical

60
00:02:20,880 --> 00:02:27,760
compute units, each holding up to 2048

61
00:02:24,600 --> 00:02:30,040
threads at once, over 270,000

62
00:02:27,760 --> 00:02:32,440
in flight across the chip.

63
00:02:30,040 --> 00:02:34,880
Not one of them is fast on its own,

64
00:02:32,440 --> 00:02:38,240
and none gets the anticipation machinery

65
00:02:34,880 --> 00:02:40,520
a CPU lavishes on a single thread.

66
00:02:38,240 --> 00:02:43,120
For this workload, that is exactly the

67
00:02:40,520 --> 00:02:45,520
right call, even if my first reaction

68
00:02:43,120 --> 00:02:48,080
was that a chip full of slow threads

69
00:02:45,520 --> 00:02:49,800
just throws performance away.

70
00:02:48,080 --> 00:02:52,680
When the job is the same operation over

71
00:02:49,800 --> 00:02:54,640
a billion elements, 100,000 slow threads

72
00:02:52,680 --> 00:02:58,040
in parallel beat a handful of fast ones

73
00:02:54,640 --> 00:02:58,040
by orders of magnitude.

74
00:03:00,320 --> 00:03:05,000
Zoom into one of those 132 compute

75
00:03:02,880 --> 00:03:07,519
units, and you're looking at a streaming

76
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:10,519
multiprocessor, the SM.

77
00:03:07,519 --> 00:03:14,400
The whole chip is one SM design stamped

78
00:03:10,519 --> 00:03:17,239
out 132 times, so understand one and you

79
00:03:14,400 --> 00:03:19,680
understand the GPU.

80
00:03:17,239 --> 00:03:23,880
The heart of an SM is its arithmetic

81
00:03:19,680 --> 00:03:26,519
units, 128 CUDA cores, the general

82
00:03:23,880 --> 00:03:28,720
purpose scalar units that each do one

83
00:03:26,519 --> 00:03:30,880
multiply add per cycle,

84
00:03:28,720 --> 00:03:32,880
plus four tensor cores that operate on

85
00:03:30,880 --> 00:03:34,400
whole matrix tiles instead of single

86
00:03:32,880 --> 00:03:37,000
values.

87
00:03:34,400 --> 00:03:39,320
The H100's headline throughput, close to

88
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:41,320
a thousand trillion operations a second,

89
00:03:39,320 --> 00:03:44,519
comes almost entirely from those four

90
00:03:41,320 --> 00:03:46,040
tensor cores, not the 128 CUDA cores

91
00:03:44,519 --> 00:03:48,760
beside them.

92
00:03:46,040 --> 00:03:51,440
We'll come back to how they do it later.

93
00:03:48,760 --> 00:03:54,000
Everything else on the SM exists to keep

94
00:03:51,440 --> 00:03:56,360
those units fed, because a math unit

95
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:58,440
waiting on data is doing nothing.

96
00:03:56,360 --> 00:03:59,960
Right against the core sits the fastest

97
00:03:58,440 --> 00:04:03,000
memory on the chip.

98
00:03:59,960 --> 00:04:05,080
A 256 kilobyte register file with single

99
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:07,720
cycle [music] access partitioned across

100
00:04:05,080 --> 00:04:10,680
every thread the SM is running.

101
00:04:07,720 --> 00:04:12,640
One level out is another 256 kilobytes

102
00:04:10,680 --> 00:04:15,840
of on-chip SRAM.

103
00:04:12,640 --> 00:04:18,320
Up to 228 of those kilobytes can be

104
00:04:15,840 --> 00:04:20,440
claimed as shared memory, a scratchpad

105
00:04:18,320 --> 00:04:22,919
the program manages by hand. So,

106
00:04:20,440 --> 00:04:27,120
cooperating threads stage the data they

107
00:04:22,919 --> 00:04:29,560
share while the rest serves as L1 cache.

108
00:04:27,120 --> 00:04:31,840
Four schedulers keep it all moving, each

109
00:04:29,560 --> 00:04:35,200
choosing a group of threads every cycle

110
00:04:31,840 --> 00:04:37,560
and issuing their next instruction.

111
00:04:35,200 --> 00:04:40,000
One more piece is worth calling out new

112
00:04:37,560 --> 00:04:42,200
with Hopper, a dedicated unit called the

113
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:44,640
Tensor Memory Accelerator.

114
00:04:42,200 --> 00:04:48,000
It's only job is to stream blocks of

115
00:04:44,640 --> 00:04:51,080
data from main memory into that SRAM in

116
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:54,520
the background. So, threads never spend

117
00:04:51,080 --> 00:04:58,720
their own instructions on the transfer.

118
00:04:54,520 --> 00:05:00,640
One SM holds up to 2048 threads. Across

119
00:04:58,720 --> 00:05:03,400
all 132,

120
00:05:00,640 --> 00:05:05,040
that is where the chip's 270,000 come

121
00:05:03,400 --> 00:05:07,760
from.

122
00:05:05,040 --> 00:05:09,600
Those groups of threads have a name.

123
00:05:07,760 --> 00:05:12,600
Warps.

124
00:05:09,600 --> 00:05:15,960
A warp is 32 threads and it's the real

125
00:05:12,600 --> 00:05:18,760
unit the hardware schedules. The 2048

126
00:05:15,960 --> 00:05:22,200
threads on an SM always move in warps of

127
00:05:18,760 --> 00:05:24,880
32, never one at a time.

128
00:05:22,200 --> 00:05:27,160
What defines a warp is that all 32

129
00:05:24,880 --> 00:05:30,720
threads execute the same instruction at

130
00:05:27,160 --> 00:05:32,440
the same moment, each on its own data.

131
00:05:30,720 --> 00:05:35,360
The hardware fetches that instruction

132
00:05:32,440 --> 00:05:38,960
once, decodes it once, and fires it

133
00:05:35,360 --> 00:05:41,280
across all 32 lanes in lockstep. Nvidia

134
00:05:38,960 --> 00:05:45,080
calls this SIMT,

135
00:05:41,280 --> 00:05:47,000
single instruction multiple threads.

136
00:05:45,080 --> 00:05:49,040
That single fact is where the GPUs

137
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:51,240
compute density comes from.

138
00:05:49,040 --> 00:05:54,440
Fetching and decoding an instruction

139
00:05:51,240 --> 00:05:56,960
costs real silicon and energy. And a

140
00:05:54,440 --> 00:05:59,440
scalar processor pays that cost for

141
00:05:56,960 --> 00:06:02,520
every operation it runs.

142
00:05:59,440 --> 00:06:05,080
A GPU pays it once per warp and spreads

143
00:06:02,520 --> 00:06:07,400
it across 32. The control overhead

144
00:06:05,080 --> 00:06:10,560
nearly disappears, which frees the chip

145
00:06:07,400 --> 00:06:12,720
to put its silicon into arithmetic.

146
00:06:10,560 --> 00:06:15,360
The SM's four schedulers work at this

147
00:06:12,720 --> 00:06:18,440
same granularity, each issuing one ready

148
00:06:15,360 --> 00:06:20,880
warp per cycle. And with up to 64 warps

149
00:06:18,440 --> 00:06:23,360
resident at once, they almost always

150
00:06:20,880 --> 00:06:27,280
have one ready to fire.

151
00:06:23,360 --> 00:06:29,360
That lockstep is also SIMT's weakness.

152
00:06:27,280 --> 00:06:31,840
A warp shares one instruction stream

153
00:06:29,360 --> 00:06:34,280
across all 32 threads, [music] so it can

154
00:06:31,840 --> 00:06:36,080
only do one thing at a time.

155
00:06:34,280 --> 00:06:38,000
What happens when the threads need to do

156
00:06:36,080 --> 00:06:41,080
different things?

157
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:43,640
Say the code hits an if else, and inside

158
00:06:41,080 --> 00:06:45,919
one warp some threads take the if while

159
00:06:43,640 --> 00:06:47,880
the rest take the else. There's only one

160
00:06:45,919 --> 00:06:50,600
instruction stream, so the hardware

161
00:06:47,880 --> 00:06:53,080
can't run both branches at once.

162
00:06:50,600 --> 00:06:55,760
It runs them back-to-back. The if path

163
00:06:53,080 --> 00:06:57,840
first with the else threads masked off,

164
00:06:55,760 --> 00:06:59,160
then the else path with the if threads

165
00:06:57,840 --> 00:07:01,520
masked.

166
00:06:59,160 --> 00:07:03,160
Each thread sits idle through the half

167
00:07:01,520 --> 00:07:06,240
it skipped.

168
00:07:03,160 --> 00:07:09,360
So a balanced if else costs you both

169
00:07:06,240 --> 00:07:11,760
paths in sequence and cuts throughput in

170
00:07:09,360 --> 00:07:13,440
half, and nesting branches only

171
00:07:11,760 --> 00:07:15,800
compounds it.

172
00:07:13,440 --> 00:07:18,680
This is warp divergence, one of the two

173
00:07:15,800 --> 00:07:21,360
cardinal sins of GPU programming.

174
00:07:18,680 --> 00:07:22,400
The other is about memory, not control

175
00:07:21,360 --> 00:07:25,080
flow.

176
00:07:22,400 --> 00:07:26,960
When the 32 threads in a warp reach for

177
00:07:25,080 --> 00:07:29,120
scattered addresses instead of

178
00:07:26,960 --> 00:07:32,040
neighboring ones, most of what the

179
00:07:29,120 --> 00:07:34,440
hardware fetches gets thrown away.

180
00:07:32,040 --> 00:07:37,440
That one is uncoalesced access, and

181
00:07:34,440 --> 00:07:37,440
we'll come back to it.

182
00:07:39,680 --> 00:07:44,040
We keep saying the math is fast and the

183
00:07:42,200 --> 00:07:46,200
data is slow.

184
00:07:44,040 --> 00:07:48,600
The reason is physical.

185
00:07:46,200 --> 00:07:50,920
The GPU's memory is a ladder of four

186
00:07:48,600 --> 00:07:54,800
levels and each step away from the

187
00:07:50,920 --> 00:07:57,760
compute units trade speed for capacity.

188
00:07:54,800 --> 00:08:01,400
At the top, right inside each SM, are

189
00:07:57,760 --> 00:08:04,080
the registers, 256 kilobytes of them,

190
00:08:01,400 --> 00:08:06,400
reachable in a single cycle. A thread's

191
00:08:04,080 --> 00:08:08,840
working values live here and getting at

192
00:08:06,400 --> 00:08:11,560
them is as good as free.

193
00:08:08,840 --> 00:08:15,520
One step down is the on-chip SRAM we met

194
00:08:11,560 --> 00:08:18,520
earlier. Up to 228 kilobytes per SM as

195
00:08:15,520 --> 00:08:21,160
shared memory plus L1, still on the SM

196
00:08:18,520 --> 00:08:24,280
and still measured in nanoseconds.

197
00:08:21,160 --> 00:08:28,080
Below that, the L2 cache, 50 megabytes

198
00:08:24,280 --> 00:08:31,320
shared across all 132 SMs, and the last

199
00:08:28,080 --> 00:08:34,640
level that still lives on the GPU die.

200
00:08:31,320 --> 00:08:36,840
Then comes HBM, high bandwidth memory,

201
00:08:34,640 --> 00:08:40,039
80 gigabytes of DRAM stacked beside the

202
00:08:36,840 --> 00:08:43,200
GPU but off the compute die itself.

203
00:08:40,039 --> 00:08:45,440
It delivers 3.35 terabytes per second,

204
00:08:43,200 --> 00:08:47,480
which sounds enormous, yet a single

205
00:08:45,440 --> 00:08:49,160
access still takes hundreds of cycles to

206
00:08:47,480 --> 00:08:51,000
come back.

207
00:08:49,160 --> 00:08:53,176
Top to bottom, that's roughly a

208
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:56,000
hundredfold drop in bandwidth and a

209
00:08:53,176 --> 00:08:58,760
[music] thousandfold jump in latency.

210
00:08:56,000 --> 00:09:00,760
Reaching down to HBM for a byte costs

211
00:08:58,760 --> 00:09:03,200
vastly more than doing arithmetic on a

212
00:09:00,760 --> 00:09:05,360
byte you already have up top and that

213
00:09:03,200 --> 00:09:07,760
asymmetry is the whole game.

214
00:09:05,360 --> 00:09:10,040
GPU programming is mostly about staging

215
00:09:07,760 --> 00:09:13,160
data up this ladder, keeping the tensor

216
00:09:10,040 --> 00:09:15,680
cores fed from the fast end.

217
00:09:13,160 --> 00:09:17,240
So, a trip to HBM costs hundreds of

218
00:09:15,680 --> 00:09:20,120
cycles.

219
00:09:17,240 --> 00:09:22,360
A CPU would dodge a wait like that by

220
00:09:20,120 --> 00:09:25,240
guessing what comes next and fetching

221
00:09:22,360 --> 00:09:27,839
the data before it's asked for.

222
00:09:25,240 --> 00:09:30,720
The GPU does none of that. How does it

223
00:09:27,839 --> 00:09:34,440
survive a stall this long?

224
00:09:30,720 --> 00:09:37,520
It doesn't avoid the stall, it hides it.

225
00:09:34,440 --> 00:09:40,120
An SM keeps up to 64 warps resident at

226
00:09:37,520 --> 00:09:43,040
once, and when one warp fires off a load

227
00:09:40,120 --> 00:09:44,960
to HBM and stalls, a scheduler just runs

228
00:09:43,040 --> 00:09:47,040
another warp that's ready.

229
00:09:44,960 --> 00:09:49,600
By the time the first warp's data comes

230
00:09:47,040 --> 00:09:51,200
back, dozens of others have taken their

231
00:09:49,600 --> 00:09:53,000
turn.

232
00:09:51,200 --> 00:09:54,040
This works because switching warps is

233
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:56,080
free.

234
00:09:54,040 --> 00:09:58,080
Every resident warp's registers stay in

235
00:09:56,080 --> 00:10:00,360
the register file the whole time, so

236
00:09:58,080 --> 00:10:02,160
there's nothing to save or restore. The

237
00:10:00,360 --> 00:10:04,160
scheduler just points at a different

238
00:10:02,160 --> 00:10:07,560
warp next cycle.

239
00:10:04,160 --> 00:10:10,240
A CPU burns real cycles to swap threads,

240
00:10:07,560 --> 00:10:12,840
and SM pays nothing.

241
00:10:10,240 --> 00:10:15,160
That's latency hiding, the GPU's whole

242
00:10:12,840 --> 00:10:17,040
answer to slow memory.

243
00:10:15,160 --> 00:10:19,520
Each warp still waits hundreds of cycles

244
00:10:17,040 --> 00:10:22,040
for HBM. The SM with enough warps in

245
00:10:19,520 --> 00:10:23,760
flight never does.

246
00:10:22,040 --> 00:10:25,800
Here's that second sin.

247
00:10:23,760 --> 00:10:28,600
And this one wastes the HBM bandwidth

248
00:10:25,800 --> 00:10:31,200
the whole chip is starved for.

249
00:10:28,600 --> 00:10:33,080
It starts with one limitation.

250
00:10:31,200 --> 00:10:35,640
The hardware can't fetch a single value

251
00:10:33,080 --> 00:10:38,040
from HBM. It moves a whole block at a

252
00:10:35,640 --> 00:10:40,880
time, a cache line.

253
00:10:38,040 --> 00:10:43,440
So, when the 32 threads in a warp want

254
00:10:40,880 --> 00:10:46,040
values that sit next to each other, they

255
00:10:43,440 --> 00:10:49,160
fall inside the same block, and one

256
00:10:46,040 --> 00:10:51,520
transaction serves the entire warp.

257
00:10:49,160 --> 00:10:54,680
That's coalesced access, the efficient

258
00:10:51,520 --> 00:10:56,680
case. Every byte moved is a byte some

259
00:10:54,680 --> 00:10:59,080
thread wanted.

260
00:10:56,680 --> 00:11:00,120
Now, scatter those 32 addresses across

261
00:10:59,080 --> 00:11:01,960
memory.

262
00:11:00,120 --> 00:11:04,240
Each thread's value lands in a different

263
00:11:01,960 --> 00:11:07,160
block, so the hardware runs a separate

264
00:11:04,240 --> 00:11:09,560
fetch for each, up to 32 transactions

265
00:11:07,160 --> 00:11:11,839
where the coalesced version needed one.

266
00:11:09,560 --> 00:11:14,080
And every block it drags in is mostly

267
00:11:11,839 --> 00:11:16,640
bytes nobody asked for, hauled across

268
00:11:14,080 --> 00:11:19,839
the bus, and thrown away.

269
00:11:16,640 --> 00:11:21,960
The arithmetic is identical either way.

270
00:11:19,839 --> 00:11:24,520
The only thing that changed is where the

271
00:11:21,960 --> 00:11:27,200
data sits in memory, and it can cost

272
00:11:24,520 --> 00:11:30,360
many times the bandwidth. On a chip this

273
00:11:27,200 --> 00:11:32,440
memory bound, data layout is as decisive

274
00:11:30,360 --> 00:11:34,400
as the math.

275
00:11:32,440 --> 00:11:36,000
Latency hiding, coalescing, the whole

276
00:11:34,400 --> 00:11:39,080
obsession with feeding the compute

277
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:41,480
units, it all traces back to one trend,

278
00:11:39,080 --> 00:11:43,440
and that trend has a name.

279
00:11:41,480 --> 00:11:45,840
The memory wall.

280
00:11:43,440 --> 00:11:48,560
Across GPU generations, the compute side

281
00:11:45,840 --> 00:11:51,080
keeps pulling away from the memory side.

282
00:11:48,560 --> 00:11:54,600
Bandwidth has grown steadily from about

283
00:11:51,080 --> 00:11:58,040
2 TB per second on the 2020 A100 to

284
00:11:54,600 --> 00:12:00,800
roughly 8 on the 2024 Blackwell B200, a

285
00:11:58,040 --> 00:12:03,080
fourfold gain in 4 years.

286
00:12:00,800 --> 00:12:05,480
But peak compute climbed faster still,

287
00:12:03,080 --> 00:12:07,360
largely because each generation added a

288
00:12:05,480 --> 00:12:09,720
lower precision math format that

289
00:12:07,360 --> 00:12:12,160
multiplies throughput. The two curves

290
00:12:09,720 --> 00:12:13,840
keep diverging, and that widening gap is

291
00:12:12,160 --> 00:12:15,680
the wall.

292
00:12:13,840 --> 00:12:18,200
How much does the memory side matter on

293
00:12:15,680 --> 00:12:20,280
its own? Nvidia effectively ran the

294
00:12:18,200 --> 00:12:23,000
experiment for us.

295
00:12:20,280 --> 00:12:25,520
The H200 uses the exact same compute die

296
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:27,680
as the H100, the same tensor cores, the

297
00:12:25,520 --> 00:12:31,480
same peak flops. The only thing they

298
00:12:27,680 --> 00:12:36,200
changed is the memory. 141 GB of faster

299
00:12:31,480 --> 00:12:37,120
HBM3e at 4.8 TB per second, up from the

300
00:12:36,200 --> 00:12:40,440
H100's [music]

301
00:12:37,120 --> 00:12:42,960
80 GB at 3.35.

302
00:12:40,440 --> 00:12:45,040
For memory-bound work, which is most LLM

303
00:12:42,960 --> 00:12:47,480
inference at the batch a single request

304
00:12:45,040 --> 00:12:50,400
sees, that upgrade alone is a genuine

305
00:12:47,480 --> 00:12:52,560
speedup. Direct evidence that inference

306
00:12:50,400 --> 00:12:54,400
is limited by bandwidth,

307
00:12:52,560 --> 00:12:56,280
not by how fast the tensor cores can

308
00:12:54,400 --> 00:12:58,480
multiply.

309
00:12:56,280 --> 00:13:00,520
Every new generation, Nvidia attacks

310
00:12:58,480 --> 00:13:02,640
this wall the same few ways. Lower

311
00:13:00,520 --> 00:13:05,400
precision, so each weight moves fewer

312
00:13:02,640 --> 00:13:07,080
bytes, faster HBM, and wider links

313
00:13:05,400 --> 00:13:08,480
between chips.

314
00:13:07,080 --> 00:13:10,320
But whether a given operation is

315
00:13:08,480 --> 00:13:12,520
actually stuck against the wall or

316
00:13:10,320 --> 00:13:14,480
limited instead by raw compute, comes

317
00:13:12,520 --> 00:13:16,560
down to a single number, arithmetic

318
00:13:14,480 --> 00:13:19,280
intensity, and the roofline we built

319
00:13:16,560 --> 00:13:21,120
around it in the last video.

320
00:13:19,280 --> 00:13:23,280
Before we leave the hardware, it all

321
00:13:21,120 --> 00:13:26,520
comes down to one number.

322
00:13:23,280 --> 00:13:28,680
That rich point, the 295 operations per

323
00:13:26,520 --> 00:13:31,880
byte that separates memory bound from

324
00:13:28,680 --> 00:13:33,360
compute bound on the H100 isn't a free

325
00:13:31,880 --> 00:13:36,120
standing number.

326
00:13:33,360 --> 00:13:38,920
It's these two hardware peaks divided.

327
00:13:36,120 --> 00:13:42,680
The tensor core's 989 trillion

328
00:13:38,920 --> 00:13:44,920
operations a second over HBM's 3.35

329
00:13:42,680 --> 00:13:46,320
terabytes a second.

330
00:13:44,920 --> 00:13:48,640
Everything we worked out about the

331
00:13:46,320 --> 00:13:50,480
roofline last time, why decode lands

332
00:13:48,640 --> 00:13:52,840
deep on the memory bound side, and

333
00:13:50,480 --> 00:13:55,080
prefill lands on the compute side, comes

334
00:13:52,840 --> 00:13:56,840
back to those two ceilings.

335
00:13:55,080 --> 00:13:58,200
The tensor cores are the compute

336
00:13:56,840 --> 00:14:02,600
ceiling.

337
00:13:58,200 --> 00:14:02,600
HBM is the bandwidth ceiling.

338
00:14:04,840 --> 00:14:09,840
Earlier, inside the SM, we said we'd

339
00:14:07,440 --> 00:14:12,880
come back to the tensor cores.

340
00:14:09,840 --> 00:14:14,760
This is how four small units per SM

341
00:14:12,880 --> 00:14:16,480
deliver almost all of the chip's

342
00:14:14,760 --> 00:14:19,720
throughput.

343
00:14:16,480 --> 00:14:22,560
A CUDA core is scalar. It multiplies two

344
00:14:19,720 --> 00:14:24,240
numbers and adds a third, one result per

345
00:14:22,560 --> 00:14:26,560
cycle.

346
00:14:24,240 --> 00:14:28,080
A tensor core doesn't deal in single

347
00:14:26,560 --> 00:14:30,200
numbers.

348
00:14:28,080 --> 00:14:32,880
In one instruction, it multiplies two

349
00:14:30,200 --> 00:14:34,960
small matrices and adds a third,

350
00:14:32,880 --> 00:14:37,320
producing a whole tile of results at

351
00:14:34,960 --> 00:14:39,640
once, where a CUDA core would grind

352
00:14:37,320 --> 00:14:41,560
through them one at a time.

353
00:14:39,640 --> 00:14:44,520
That operation is matrix multiply

354
00:14:41,560 --> 00:14:46,400
accumulate, MMA, and it's all a tensor

355
00:14:44,520 --> 00:14:48,839
core does.

356
00:14:46,400 --> 00:14:51,760
That narrowness is the point.

357
00:14:48,839 --> 00:14:54,640
A transformer spends well over 90% of

358
00:14:51,760 --> 00:14:57,200
its arithmetic inside matrix multiplies.

359
00:14:54,640 --> 00:14:59,920
So, a unit that does nothing else very

360
00:14:57,200 --> 00:15:01,800
fast is the highest leverage silicon

361
00:14:59,920 --> 00:15:05,200
Nvidia can build.

362
00:15:01,800 --> 00:15:06,760
The H100 has 528 of them,

363
00:15:05,200 --> 00:15:08,160
four per SM,

364
00:15:06,760 --> 00:15:10,920
and they produce the overwhelming

365
00:15:08,160 --> 00:15:14,160
majority of its peak throughput.

366
00:15:10,920 --> 00:15:16,320
One caveat, that peak is theoretical,

367
00:15:14,160 --> 00:15:18,440
the core's perfectly fed.

368
00:15:16,320 --> 00:15:21,680
Sustained throughput in real kernels

369
00:15:18,440 --> 00:15:24,680
typically reaches 50 to 80% of peak for

370
00:15:21,680 --> 00:15:27,800
well-tuned code and considerably less

371
00:15:24,680 --> 00:15:29,480
for unoptimized implementations.

372
00:15:27,800 --> 00:15:31,880
There's a second level though, built

373
00:15:29,480 --> 00:15:33,680
into the hardware rather than the code,

374
00:15:31,880 --> 00:15:35,560
letting these cores run at lower

375
00:15:33,680 --> 00:15:38,080
precision.

376
00:15:35,560 --> 00:15:40,360
Lower precision is that second lever,

377
00:15:38,080 --> 00:15:42,640
and it pays [music] off twice.

378
00:15:40,360 --> 00:15:44,680
Storing a number in fewer bits lets the

379
00:15:42,640 --> 00:15:46,480
tensor cores push more of them through

380
00:15:44,680 --> 00:15:48,760
each second,

381
00:15:46,480 --> 00:15:50,520
and leaves fewer bytes to haul across

382
00:15:48,760 --> 00:15:53,040
the memory bus.

383
00:15:50,520 --> 00:15:55,320
More compute and less memory traffic

384
00:15:53,040 --> 00:15:57,640
from the same silicon.

385
00:15:55,320 --> 00:16:00,400
So, every tensor core generation has

386
00:15:57,640 --> 00:16:02,680
added a lower precision format.

387
00:16:00,400 --> 00:16:06,640
Volta started with 16-bit floats in

388
00:16:02,680 --> 00:16:09,760
2017, Hopper brought 8-bits in 2022, and

389
00:16:06,640 --> 00:16:11,640
Blackwell added 4-bits in 2024. Each

390
00:16:09,760 --> 00:16:13,880
having of the bits roughly doubles the

391
00:16:11,640 --> 00:16:16,480
throughput.

392
00:16:13,880 --> 00:16:18,240
Why can a model tolerate this?

393
00:16:16,480 --> 00:16:20,960
A floating-point number splits into an

394
00:16:18,240 --> 00:16:24,080
exponent, which sets its range, and a

395
00:16:20,960 --> 00:16:26,080
mantissa, which sets its fine precision.

396
00:16:24,080 --> 00:16:28,400
Deep learning leans on range while

397
00:16:26,080 --> 00:16:31,800
staying forgiving about precision.

398
00:16:28,400 --> 00:16:33,040
BF16, a 16-bit format, is the clean

399
00:16:31,800 --> 00:16:35,880
example.

400
00:16:33,040 --> 00:16:38,560
It keeps all eight of FP32's exponent

401
00:16:35,880 --> 00:16:41,080
bits, so the range is identical, and

402
00:16:38,560 --> 00:16:42,080
cuts the mantissa from 23 bits down to

403
00:16:41,080 --> 00:16:44,440
seven.

404
00:16:42,080 --> 00:16:46,800
Training barely notices.

405
00:16:44,440 --> 00:16:49,000
And there's a safeguard, the multiplies

406
00:16:46,800 --> 00:16:52,440
run in low precision, but the results

407
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:54,120
accumulate back in FP32, so errors don't

408
00:16:52,440 --> 00:16:56,040
pile up.

409
00:16:54,120 --> 00:16:59,160
One caution on the marketing,

410
00:16:56,040 --> 00:17:01,160
when Nvidia headlines a 9x speedup, that

411
00:16:59,160 --> 00:17:04,480
blends the precision gain with faster

412
00:17:01,160 --> 00:17:06,680
interconnects and ideal benchmarks.

413
00:17:04,480 --> 00:17:11,120
The honest per generation gain on real

414
00:17:06,680 --> 00:17:11,120
work is more like 2 to 4x.

415
00:17:13,439 --> 00:17:18,400
A frontier model doesn't fit on one GPU.

416
00:17:17,040 --> 00:17:21,760
Its weights run to hundreds of

417
00:17:18,400 --> 00:17:23,880
gigabytes, well past the 80 or 140 a

418
00:17:21,760 --> 00:17:26,280
single chip holds. So, it gets split

419
00:17:23,880 --> 00:17:27,839
across many GPUs that then have to talk

420
00:17:26,280 --> 00:17:29,560
constantly.

421
00:17:27,839 --> 00:17:31,840
How fast they can talk is the next

422
00:17:29,560 --> 00:17:33,320
constraint.

423
00:17:31,840 --> 00:17:34,920
There are two ways to wire them

424
00:17:33,320 --> 00:17:37,600
together.

425
00:17:34,920 --> 00:17:40,160
Scale-up packs many GPUs into one box or

426
00:17:37,600 --> 00:17:42,680
rack joined by NVLink, Nvidia's

427
00:17:40,160 --> 00:17:45,200
dedicated [music] GPU to GPU link, and a

428
00:17:42,680 --> 00:17:47,400
crossbar called NV switch that lets

429
00:17:45,200 --> 00:17:48,760
every GPU reach every other at full

430
00:17:47,400 --> 00:17:52,080
speed.

431
00:17:48,760 --> 00:17:57,520
NVLink is fast and keeps getting faster.

432
00:17:52,080 --> 00:18:02,640
600 GB/s per GPU on the A100, 900 on the

433
00:17:57,520 --> 00:18:05,200
H100, 1.8 TB/s on Blackwell.

434
00:18:02,640 --> 00:18:07,720
Scale-out is the other way. Separate

435
00:18:05,200 --> 00:18:09,880
boxes linked by ordinary data center

436
00:18:07,720 --> 00:18:12,160
networking, InfiniBand [music]

437
00:18:09,880 --> 00:18:14,640
at a few hundred gigabits a second,

438
00:18:12,160 --> 00:18:16,600
roughly an order of magnitude slower per

439
00:18:14,640 --> 00:18:18,960
byte.

440
00:18:16,600 --> 00:18:21,320
Why does that gap matter? Tensor

441
00:18:18,960 --> 00:18:23,800
parallelism splits each layer's matrix

442
00:18:21,320 --> 00:18:26,280
multiply across GPUs, so they have to

443
00:18:23,800 --> 00:18:28,240
exchange results at every layer dozens

444
00:18:26,280 --> 00:18:31,240
of times per token.

445
00:18:28,240 --> 00:18:34,480
Inside an NVLink domain, that's cheap.

446
00:18:31,240 --> 00:18:36,560
Across InfiniBand, it's the bottleneck.

447
00:18:34,480 --> 00:18:37,840
So, Nvidia keeps growing the fast

448
00:18:36,560 --> 00:18:41,240
domain.

449
00:18:37,840 --> 00:18:44,080
A DGX box held eight GPUs on both the

450
00:18:41,240 --> 00:18:47,800
A100 and H100.

451
00:18:44,080 --> 00:18:49,920
The Blackwell GB200 NVL72 puts 72 in one

452
00:18:47,800 --> 00:18:52,240
liquid-cooled rack, all in a single

453
00:18:49,920 --> 00:18:53,800
NVLink domain. So, far more of the model

454
00:18:52,240 --> 00:18:56,960
fits in the fast neighborhood before

455
00:18:53,800 --> 00:18:58,880
you're forced out onto the slow fabric.

456
00:18:56,960 --> 00:19:00,480
One last thing, because it's easy to

457
00:18:58,880 --> 00:19:01,960
come away thinking this is all about

458
00:19:00,480 --> 00:19:03,480
Nvidia.

459
00:19:01,960 --> 00:19:05,440
It isn't.

460
00:19:03,480 --> 00:19:07,680
The physics we've covered, the memory

461
00:19:05,440 --> 00:19:10,160
wall, the value of low precision,

462
00:19:07,680 --> 00:19:12,160
scaling up before out, applies to anyone

463
00:19:10,160 --> 00:19:14,200
building these chips, and there are real

464
00:19:12,160 --> 00:19:16,800
alternatives.

465
00:19:14,200 --> 00:19:19,320
Google's TPU throws out the SM model

466
00:19:16,800 --> 00:19:21,480
entirely and builds the chip around one

467
00:19:19,320 --> 00:19:23,520
giant systolic array.

468
00:19:21,480 --> 00:19:25,320
A fixed grid of multiply accumulate

469
00:19:23,520 --> 00:19:27,400
cells that data flows through

470
00:19:25,320 --> 00:19:29,680
rhythmically with no trips back to a

471
00:19:27,400 --> 00:19:31,320
register file in between.

472
00:19:29,680 --> 00:19:34,080
That makes it well suited to dense

473
00:19:31,320 --> 00:19:36,480
matrix multiply and not much else, and

474
00:19:34,080 --> 00:19:38,120
it's rentable for now only on Google

475
00:19:36,480 --> 00:19:40,520
Cloud.

476
00:19:38,120 --> 00:19:42,400
AMD's MI300X

477
00:19:40,520 --> 00:19:44,720
is closer to a GPU,

478
00:19:42,400 --> 00:19:48,720
and on paper it beats the H100 where it

479
00:19:44,720 --> 00:19:51,560
matters here. 192 GB of HBM, more than

480
00:19:48,720 --> 00:19:53,600
double. So, a model that needs two H100s

481
00:19:51,560 --> 00:19:55,240
can fit on one.

482
00:19:53,600 --> 00:19:57,480
And yet most teams still reach for

483
00:19:55,240 --> 00:19:58,960
Nvidia, and the deciding factor is

484
00:19:57,480 --> 00:20:01,200
software.

485
00:19:58,960 --> 00:20:03,080
CUDA has had almost two decades to build

486
00:20:01,200 --> 00:20:05,840
up the libraries and tooling that all of

487
00:20:03,080 --> 00:20:08,960
AI now sits on, and ROCm, AMD's

488
00:20:05,840 --> 00:20:11,080
equivalent, is still catching up.

489
00:20:08,960 --> 00:20:14,200
For most teams, that maturity is the

490
00:20:11,080 --> 00:20:15,840
real binding constraint.

491
00:20:14,200 --> 00:20:17,440
And that's a good place to end because

492
00:20:15,840 --> 00:20:19,440
the software is exactly where we go

493
00:20:17,440 --> 00:20:21,480
next.

494
00:20:19,440 --> 00:20:23,680
We spent this video on the machine. The

495
00:20:21,480 --> 00:20:26,880
next one is about the kernels, the code

496
00:20:23,680 --> 00:20:26,880
that makes it fast.

